There is no simple algebraic formula that outputs every prime number sequentially, though mathematicians have proven several exact algorithms (sieves) and complex formulas that generate all prime numbers.

1. Proven Algorithms (Sieves) That Generate All Primes

In computer science and mathematics, the most reliable ways to generate every prime number up to a limit N are prime sieves:
• Sieve of Eratosthenes (c. 240 BCE): The classic, proven method. You list numbers from 2 to N, circle the first unmarked number (2), and cross out all of its multiples. Repeating this process leaves 100% of the prime numbers up to N.
• Sieve of Sundaram (1934): Lists integers up to n and eliminates all numbers of the form i + j + 2ij. Doubling the remaining numbers and adding 1 produces every odd prime up to 2n + 1.
• Sieve of Atkin (2003): A modern, faster algorithm that uses quadratic forms (like 4x² + y²) and modulo-60 remainders to mark primes, making it more efficient than Eratosthenes for very large ranges.

2. Exact Mathematical Formulas (Slow, But Proven)

While no simple polynomial (like n² + n + 41) produces only primes forever, mathematicians have proven exact formulas that yield every prime—though they are far too slow for practical computing:
• Wilson’s Theorem (1770): A number n > 1 is prime if and only if (n - 1)! + 1 is evenly divisible by n. Turning this into a formula gives the n-th prime, though calculating factorials for large numbers is computationally massive.
• Mills’ Constant (1947): Proven to exist, there is a specific real number A ≈ 1.30637788... such that rounding down \(\lfloor A^{3^n} \rfloor\) produces a prime number for every positive integer \(n = 1, 2, 3, \dots\) (though finding A requires knowing the primes beforehand).
• Rowland’s Recurrence (2008): Starting with a₁ = 7 and defining \(a_n = a_{n-1} + \gcd(n, a_{n-1})\), the differences \(a_n - a_{n-1}\) always equal either 1 or a prime number.
• Willans' Formula (1964): Combines Wilson’s theorem with trigonometric functions to give an exact, closed-form equation for the n-th prime \(p_{n}\).

3. Record-Hunting Generators (Specific Families of Primes)

To find the largest known primes (millions of digits long), mathematicians use targeted tests rather than generating every prime in order:
• Mersenne Primes (\(2^p - 1\)): Tested using the Lucas–Lehmer test, which has discovered nearly all of the largest known prime numbers.
