❓: In search of a set of Prime number Theorems & Equations 🕯️

Here are the foundational theorems and equations that define how prime numbers behave, group together, and distribute across infinity.

1. The Building-Block Theorems

These establish the core rules of what primes are and how many exist:
• Euclid’s Theorem (c. 300 BCE): There are infinitely many prime numbers. For any finite list of primes, multiplying them together and adding 1 creates a new number that cannot be divided by any prime on that list.
• Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic: Every whole number greater than 1 is either a prime itself or can be written as a unique product of prime numbers (ignoring order). For example, \(12 = 2^2 \times 3\), and no other combination of primes can ever equal 12.

2. Distribution & Counting Equations

While individual primes look unpredictable, these proven equations accurately count how many primes appear up to a large number \(x\):
• The Prime Number Theorem (1896): Describes the overall density of primes. The number of primes less than or equal to \(x\), written as \(\pi(x)\), gets closer and closer to:
\(\pi (x)\approx \frac{x}{\ln (x)}\)
(where \(\ln(x)\) is the natural logarithm of \(x\)).
• Logarithmic Integral Approximation: An even sharper proven approximation for counting primes up to \(x\):
\(\pi (x)\approx \text{Li}(x)=\int _{2}^{x}\frac{dt}{\ln (t)}\)
• Rosser’s Theorem (1939): Gives a strict lower bound for the \(n\)-th prime number (\(p_{n}\)), proving that:
\(p_{n}>n\ln (n)\)

3. Modular Arithmetic & Primality Equations

These equations govern remainders when dividing by prime numbers and form the backbone of modern cryptography:
• Fermat’s Little Theorem (1640): If \(p\) is a prime number and \(a\) is any integer not divisible by \(p\), then raising \(a\) to the power of \(p - 1\) always leaves a remainder of 1 when divided by \(p\):
\(a^{p-1}\equiv 1\mathinner{\;\left(\mod \,p\right)}\)
• Wilson’s Theorem (1770): A natural number \(n > 1\) is prime if and only if:
\((n-1)!\equiv -1\mathinner{\;\left(\mod \,n\right)}\)
• Dirichlet’s Theorem on Arithmetic Progressions (1837): Any sequence of numbers \(a + nd\) (where \(a\) and \(d\) share no common factors) contains infinitely many prime numbers (for example, there are infinitely many primes ending in the digit 7).

4. Gap & Pattern Theorems

• Bertrand’s Postulate (1852): For any whole number \(n > 1\), there is always at least one prime number strictly between \(n\) and \(2n\).
• Green–Tao Theorem (2004): The sequence of prime numbers contains arbitrarily long arithmetic progressions—meaning you can find equally spaced sequences of primes of any finite length you choose (such as 3, 5, 7 or 7, 37, 67, 97, 127, 157).
