3. The Gutzwiller Trace Formula: Closed Orbits as Prime Numbers

Developed by Martin Gutzwiller in 1971, the Gutzwiller trace formula connects the quantum energy levels of a chaotic system to the closed, repeating paths (periodic orbits) a classical particle can take inside that system. [1] (https://empslocal.ex.ac.uk/people/staff/mrwatkin/zeta/GTF-RZF.htm), [2] (https://inspirehep.net/files/20b84db59eace6a7f90fc38516f530ee)

A. The Anatomy of the Trace Formula

In quantum mechanics, the density of energy states \(d(E)\) splits into two parts: a smooth average background \(\bar{d}(E)\) and a fluctuating, oscillatory wave part \(d_{\text{osc}}(E)\). Gutzwiller showed that those quantum fluctuations are built by summing waves over every classical periodic orbit: [1] (https://arxiv.org/pdf/1101.3116), [2] (https://arxiv.org/pdf/nlin/0212042), [3] (http://large.stanford.edu/courses/2020/ph470/rahman/)
\(d_{\text{osc}}(E)\approx \frac{1}{\pi \hbar }\sum _{\gamma }\sum _{k=1}^{\infty }\frac{T_{\gamma }}{\sqrt{|{}\det (M_{\gamma }^{k}-I)|{}}}\cos \left(\frac{kS_{\gamma }(E)}{\hbar }-\frac{\pi }{2}k\mu _{\gamma }\right)\)
• \(\gamma \) (Primitive Orbits): Fundamental closed loops that do not retrace themselves.
• \(k\) (Repetitions): How many times the particle traverses the same loop \(\gamma \).
• \(T_{\gamma }\) and \(S_{\gamma }\): The period (time) and classical action of the orbit.
• \(M_{\gamma }\): The monodromy matrix, measuring how unstable or chaotic the orbit is. [1] (https://theory.uchicago.edu/~ejm/course/JournalClub/alex_gutzwiller_formula.pdf), [2] (https://empslocal.ex.ac.uk/people/staff/mrwatkin/zeta/GTF-RZF.htm), [3] (https://arxiv.org/pdf/nlin/0212042), [4] (http://large.stanford.edu/courses/2020/ph470/rahman/)

