In physics, a symmetry group describes transformations (like rotating in space or changing internal quantum phases) that leave the laws of nature unchanged. A representation is a way of writing those abstract symmetry transformations as matrices acting on a vector space of quantum states.
• Reducible vs. Irreducible ("Atomic" Representations): If a matrix representation can be block-diagonalized into smaller, independent matrices, it is reducible—meaning it describes a composite system that splits into separate parts. If it cannot be broken down any further, it is an irreducible representation (irrep)—an indivisible quantum "atom."
