E7. Persistent Homology: Extending Smith Normal Form to Noisy Point Clouds

In real-world data—such as 3D LiDAR scans, protein folding coordinates, or cosmic galaxy distributions—you do not start with a clean mesh. You start with a noisy cloud of discrete points where a single choice of connection radius might miss a hole or create fake "noise" loops.

1. Building a Filtration (Growing Spheres Around Points)

Instead of guessing one radius \(\epsilon \), Persistent Homology (pioneered by Edelsbrunner, Letscher, Zomorodian, and Carlsson in the early 2000s) grows spheres of radius \(\epsilon \) around every data point continuously from \(\epsilon = 0\) to \(\epsilon = \infty\):
• Whenever two spheres touch, an edge (1-simplex) is added.
• Whenever three spheres overlap pairwise, a triangle (2-simplex) is filled in.
• This creates a nested sequence of growing shapes called a filtration (such as a Vietoris–Rips complex):
\(K_{\epsilon _{1}}\subseteq K_{\epsilon _{2}}\subseteq K_{\epsilon _{3}}\subseteq \cdots \subseteq K_{\epsilon _{m}}\)

2. From Static SNF to the "Elder Rule" Matrix Reduction

Standard Smith Normal Form works on a single static boundary matrix \(\partial _{k}\), scrambling row and column orders freely. In Persistent Homology, columns are ordered chronologically by the exact radius \(\epsilon \) at which each simplex is born:
• Restricted Column Operations: To preserve the timeline of birth and death, the persistence algorithm computes a graded Smith Normal Form using left-to-right column additions only (no swapping columns out of chronological order).
• Pairing Births and Deaths: As the matrix reduces, each pivot entry pairs a simplex born at radius \(\epsilon _{\text{birth}}\) (which creates a hole) with a higher-dimensional simplex at radius \(\epsilon _{\text{death}}\) (which fills that hole in).

3. Persistence Barcodes & Diagrams: Filtering Out Noise

Every topological feature is plotted as a bar or point \((\epsilon_{\text{birth}}, \epsilon_{\text{death}})\):
• Short Bars (\(\epsilon_{\text{death}} - \epsilon_{\text{birth}}\) is tiny): A tiny loop formed by three noisy points that immediately gets filled in by a triangle as \(\epsilon \) grows slightly. This is statistical noise.
• Long Bars (\(\epsilon_{\text{death}} - \epsilon_{\text{birth}}\) is huge): A genuine macro-structure (like the central ring of a donut, a hollow protein pocket, or a cosmic void between galaxy filaments) that survives across a wide range of scales. This is true geometric signal.
(Note: Because matrix reduction over integers \(\mathbb{Z}\) can become computationally slow for millions of points, modern Topological Data Analysis software typically computes Persistent Homology over prime finite fields \(\mathbb{F}_{p}\), such as \(\mathbb{F}_{2}\) or \(\mathbb{F}_{3}\), where torsion twists modulo \(p\) can still be detected by comparing results across different primes!)

