E9. How the 8-Dimensional Octonions (\(\mathbb{O}\)) Generate Standard Model Symmetries

By Hurwitz’s Theorem (1898), there are only four normed division algebras in all of mathematics—number systems where you can add, subtract, multiply, and divide without zero divisors:
1. Real numbers (\(\mathbb{R}\), 1D): Ordered, commutative, associative.
2. Complex numbers (\(\mathbb{C}\), 2D): Loses ordering; commutative, associative.
3. Quaternions (\(\mathbb{H}\), 4D): Loses commutativity (\(ij = -ji\)); associative.
4. Octonions (\(\mathbb{O}\), 8D): Loses associativity (\((ab)c \neq a(bc)\)); spanned by \(1\) real unit and \(7\) imaginary units (\(e_1, e_2, \dots, e_7\)) governed by the Fano plane.
Physicists and mathematicians—notably Geoffrey Dixon, John Baez, and Cohl Furey—demonstrated that the Standard Model gauge group \(SU(3)_C \times SU(2)_L \times U(1)_Y\) emerges naturally from the Dixon algebra \(\mathbb{R} \otimes \mathbb{C} \otimes \mathbb{H} \otimes \mathbb{O}\) (or complexified octonions \(\mathbb{C} \otimes \mathbb{O}\)):

1. Fixing One Imaginary Axis Yields \(SU(3)_C\) (Color Symmetry)

