• If you pick one imaginary octonion unit—say \(e_{7}\)—to play the role of the complex imaginary unit \(i\) (defining a preferred complex direction inside the 8D space), the subgroup of \(G_{2}\) that leaves \(e_{7}\) fixed is precisely \(SU(3)\) (8-dimensional)!
• The remaining 6 imaginary units split into a 3-dimensional complex triplet \((e_1 + i e_2,\; e_3 + i e_4,\; e_5 + i e_6)\) and its anti-triplet—corresponding directly to the Red, Green, and Blue color charges of quarks!

2. Clifford Algebras & Ideals Yield One Full Generation of Fermions

