E11. Max Tegmark’s Mathematical Universe Hypothesis (MUH)

Proposed by MIT cosmologist Max Tegmark, the Mathematical Universe Hypothesis takes Galileo’s claim that "the book of nature is written in the language of mathematics" to its most radical logical conclusion: our external physical reality does not merely use mathematics—it is a mathematical structure.

1. The Core Argument: Removing "Human Baggage"

Tegmark starts from the External Reality Hypothesis (that an external physical reality exists completely independent of human beings) and argues as follows:
• Any theory of physics still contains "human baggage"—words, intuitive metaphors, and labels like "proton," "space," or "electric field."
• If you strip away every human word and retain only the pure relational rules (the equations, symmetry groups, and topological invariants), what remains is an abstract mathematical structure—a set of abstract entities with relations between them, independent of what symbols we use to name them.
• Therefore, Tegmark argues, existence in physics and existence in mathematics are identical: every consistent mathematical structure exists physically (forming the Level IV Multiverse).

2. The Philosophical & Scientific Debate

While compelling to many theoretical physicists, MUH sits at the center of intense philosophical and scientific debate:

