To build a processor on a flat silicon or sapphire wafer (used in Google’s Willow and Sycamore chips, as well as IBM’s Heavy-Hex layouts), engineers cut the torus open and lay it flat as a 2D square patch with two distinct types of boundaries (often called rough and smooth boundaries).
• Relative Homology (\(H_1(M, \partial M; \mathbb{Z}_2)\)): Instead of encoding qubits in closed loops that wrap around a donut hole, the planar surface code uses relative homology—encoding a logical qubit in strings of operators that stretch from one boundary edge all the way across the chip to the opposite boundary edge.
• Trading Qubits for Fabrication Simplicity: Cutting the torus open reduces the first Betti number from β₁ = 2 to β₁ = 1 (encoding 1 logical qubit per 2D patch), while keeping the high noise threshold (~1%) and simple nearest-neighbor wiring that make 2D superconducting chips possible today.
• Lattice Surgery (Computing Without Moving Qubits): Instead of physically braiding holes around each other, modern processors perform logical gates by dynamically merging and splitting adjacent surface-code patches along their boundaries—essentially performing real-time topological surgery on the chip.

