As your code distance d grows (making the grid wider to suppress errors), the 2D Surface Code hits a fundamental geometric limit known as the Bravyi–Poulin–Terhal (BPT) bound:
• In any 2D local lattice, encoding k logical qubits with error-correcting distance d requires at least \(n \propto k \cdot d^2\) physical qubits.
• Because k = 1 per patch, protecting a single logical qubit against realistic noise levels requires hundreds to thousands of physical qubits (n ~ d²)—an enormous hardware overhead for running large-scale quantum algorithms.

3. qLDPC Codes: Escaping Flat 2D Space

To break free from the n ~ d² overhead, researchers turned to Quantum Low-Density Parity-Check (qLDPC) codes—most notably Bivariate Bicycle (BB) codes (pioneered by IBM) and Quantum Tanner / Fiber Bundle codes.
qLDPC codes keep the exact same Abelian chain-complex and homology math (\(\partial_1 \circ \partial_2 = 0\)) as the Toric Code, but generalize the underlying geometry:
• From 2D Grids to Higher-Dimensional Expanders: Instead of restricting qubits to touch only their 4 nearest neighbors on a flat plane, qLDPC codes connect qubits across non-local, higher-dimensional hypergraph products or Cayley graphs (requiring long-range couplers or reconfigurable neutral atoms, like QuEra’s architecture).
• Torus with Many Handles (High-Genus Surfaces): Mathematically, moving from a standard torus (genus g = 1, β₁ = 2) to a hyperbolic surface with dozens of "handles" (genus \(g \gg 1\), β₁ = 2g) allows a single interconnected block of physical qubits to hold dozens of logical qubits simultaneously.
